---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="How to Choose Wire Mesh for Industrial Filtration — Complete Buyer Guide"
  description="Learn how to select the right wire mesh for filtration: material, mesh size, micron rating, weave type, and standards. Includes spec tables and factory sourcing tips."
  lang="en"
>
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-8">
    <nav class="text-sm text-theme-secondary mb-6">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-theme-primary">Home</a>
      <span class="mx-2">›</span>
      <a href="/blog/" class="hover:text-theme-primary">Blog</a>
      <span class="mx-2">›</span>
      <span>Wire Mesh for Filtration</span>
    </nav>

    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">
      How to Choose Wire Mesh for Industrial Filtration
    </h1>

    <p class="text-theme-secondary text-lg mb-8">
      Last updated: September 2026 | 12 min read
    </p>

    <!-- Answer First -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
      <p class="text-theme-primary text-lg leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Quick answer:</strong> For industrial filtration, choose <a href="/products/stainless-steel-wire-mesh/" class="text-theme-primary underline">stainless steel wire mesh</a> (304 or 316) with the correct micron rating for your particle size. Use plain weave for fine filtration (20–635 mesh), twill weave for higher strength, and dutch weave for very fine filtration (1–25 micron). Always specify wire diameter, mesh count, and material grade when requesting quotes.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- TOC -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Table of Contents</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-theme-secondary">
        <li><a href="#material" class="hover:text-theme-primary">1. Material Selection: 304 vs 316 vs Others</a></li>
        <li><a href="#micron" class="hover:text-theme-primary">2. Mesh Size vs Micron Rating</a></li>
        <li><a href="#weave" class="hover:text-theme-primary">3. Weave Types and When to Use Them</a></li>
        <li><a href="#standards" class="hover:text-theme-primary">4. Industry Standards (ASTM, ISO)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#applications" class="hover:text-theme-primary">5. Application-Specific Recommendations</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sourcing" class="hover:text-theme-primary">6. Sourcing from Factories: What to Specify</a></li>
        <li><a href="#faq" class="hover:text-theme-primary">7. FAQ</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Section 1: Material -->
    <section id="material" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">1. Material Selection: 304 vs 316 vs Others</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        The material determines corrosion resistance, temperature tolerance, and cost. For industrial filtration, stainless steel is the default choice—but which grade?
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Grade</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Corrosion Resistance</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Max Temp</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Cost</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Best For</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">304 SS</td>
              <td class="p-3">Good</td>
              <td class="p-3">800°C (1472°F)</td>
              <td class="p-3">$</td>
              <td class="p-3">Water, air, general filtration</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">316 SS</td>
              <td class="p-3">Excellent (chloride resistant)</td>
              <td class="p-3">800°C (1472°F)</td>
              <td class="p-3">$$</td>
              <td class="p-3">Marine, chemical, pharma</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">316L SS</td>
              <td class="p-3">Excellent + weld resistant</td>
              <td class="p-3">800°C (1472°F)</td>
              <td class="p-3">$$$</td>
              <td class="p-3">Welded assemblies, medical</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Monel 400</td>
              <td class="p-3">Superior (acid resistant)</td>
              <td class="p-3">480°C (896°F)</td>
              <td class="p-3">$$$$</td>
              <td class="p-3">Hydrofluoric acid, seawater</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Inconel 600</td>
              <td class="p-3">Excellent (oxidation resistant)</td>
              <td class="p-3">1090°C (1994°F)</td>
              <td class="p-3">$$$$</td>
              <td class="p-3">High-temp gas filtration</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Rule of Thumb</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary">
          If your application involves <strong>chlorides</strong> (seawater, brine, certain chemicals), upgrade from 304 to <strong>316</strong>. The 2–3% molybdenum in 316 prevents pitting corrosion that destroys 304. For most other industrial uses, 304 is sufficient and saves 20–30% on material cost.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        <strong>Avoid these mistakes:</strong>
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li>Using galvanized mesh for liquid filtration—zinc coating degrades and contaminates the fluid</li>
        <li>Choosing 304 for marine environments—it will rust within months</li>
        <li>Specifying "stainless steel" without a grade—factories may substitute 201 (low nickel, poor corrosion resistance)</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 2: Mesh Size -->
    <section id="micron" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">2. Mesh Size vs Micron Rating</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Mesh count (threads per inch) and micron rating (particle size in micrometers) are related but not interchangeable. A 100-mesh screen can have different micron ratings depending on wire diameter.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Mesh Count</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Typical Micron Rating</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Wire Diameter (mm)</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Open Area %</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Application</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3">20</td>
              <td class="p-3">850 μm</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.40</td>
              <td class="p-3">58%</td>
              <td class="p-3">Coarse pre-filtration</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3">40</td>
              <td class="p-3">425 μm</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.25</td>
              <td class="p-3">51%</td>
              <td class="p-3">General industrial</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3">60</td>
              <td class="p-3">250 μm</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.16</td>
              <td class="p-3">47%</td>
              <td class="p-3">Fine filtration</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3">80</td>
              <td class="p-3">180 μm</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.12</td>
              <td class="p-3">44%</td>
              <td class="p-3">Water treatment</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3">100</td>
              <td class="p-3">150 μm</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.10</td>
              <td class="p-3">42%</td>
              <td class="p-3">Pharmaceutical</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3">200</td>
              <td class="p-3">75 μm</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.05</td>
              <td class="p-3">38%</td>
              <td class="p-3">Fine chemical</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3">400</td>
              <td class="p-3">38 μm</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.03</td>
              <td class="p-3">32%</td>
              <td class="p-3">Ultra-filtration</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Key Insight</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary">
          <strong>Open area percentage</strong> determines flow rate. A 100-mesh screen with 0.10mm wire has ~42% open area; with 0.08mm wire, it's ~50%. For high-viscosity fluids or high-flow applications, specify thinner wire or lower mesh count to prevent pressure drop.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Use our <a href="/charts/mesh-size-chart/" class="text-theme-primary underline">interactive mesh size chart</a> to convert between mesh count, micron, and wire gauge instantly.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 3: Weave Types -->
    <section id="weave" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">3. Weave Types and When to Use Them</h2>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-6">
        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-3">Plain Weave</h3>
          <ul class="text-theme-secondary text-sm space-y-2">
            <li>• Simple over-under pattern</li>
            <li> • Mesh range: 1–635 mesh</li>
            <li> • Wire diameter: 0.025–2.0mm</li>
            <li> • <strong>Best for:</strong> General filtration, standard applications</li>
            <li> • <strong>Advantage:</strong> Lowest cost, consistent aperture</li>
            <li> • <strong>Limitation:</strong> Lower strength at fine mesh</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-3">Twill Weave</h3>
          <ul class="text-theme-secondary text-sm space-y-2">
            <li>• Wire passes over 2, under 2</li>
            <li> • Mesh range: 20–400 mesh</li>
            <li> • Wire diameter: 0.03–0.5mm</li>
            <li> • <strong>Best for:</strong> High-strength filtration, heavy loading</li>
            <li> • <strong>Advantage:</strong> Higher tensile strength, better stability</li>
            <li> • <strong>Limitation:</strong> Slightly higher cost</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-3">Dutch Weave</h3>
          <ul class="text-theme-secondary text-sm space-y-2">
            <li>• Thick warp + thin weft wires</li>
            <li> • Micron range: 1–100 μm</li>
            <li> • No mesh count (micron only)</li>
            <li> • <strong>Best for:</strong> Very fine filtration, high pressure</li>
            <li> • <strong>Advantage:</strong> Absolute filtration rating, high dirt capacity</li>
            <li> • <strong>Limitation:</strong> Low flow rate, requires support</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Selection Guide</h3>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-theme">
                <th class="text-left p-2 text-theme-primary">Filtration Range</th>
                <th class="text-left p-2 text-theme-primary">Recommended Weave</th>
                <th class="text-left p-2 text-theme-primary">Example</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
              <tr class="border-b border-theme">
                <td class="p-2">Coarse (>500 μm)</td>
                <td class="p-2">Plain weave, low mesh</td>
                <td class="p-2">10–20 mesh</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-theme">
                <td class="p-2">Medium (100–500 μm)</td>
                <td class="p-2">Plain or twill weave</td>
                <td class="p-2">40–100 mesh</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-theme">
                <td class="p-2">Fine (25–100 μm)</td>
                <td class="p-2">Twill weave</td>
                <td class="p-2">150–250 mesh</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-2">Ultra-fine (<25 μm)</td>
                <td class="p-2">Dutch weave</td>
                <td class="p-2">5–25 μm absolute</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 4: Standards -->
    <section id="standards" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">4. Industry Standards (ASTM, ISO)</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Specifying the correct standard ensures you receive consistent, testable product. The most common standards for filtration wire mesh:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Standard</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Scope</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Region</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Key Test</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">ASTM E11</td>
              <td class="p-3">Woven wire test sieve cloth</td>
              <td class="p-3">US/Global</td>
              <td class="p-3">Aperture size, wire diameter</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">ASTM E2016</td>
              <td class="p-3">Industrial woven wire cloth</td>
              <td class="p-3">US/Global</td>
              <td class="p-3">Tensile strength, elongation</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">ISO 9044</td>
              <td class="p-3">Industrial woven wire cloth</td>
              <td class="p-3">Europe/International</td>
              <td class="p-3">Mesh count, wire diameter, open area</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">ISO 3310</td>
              <td class="p-3">Test sieves</td>
              <td class="p-3">Europe/International</td>
              <td class="p-3">Sieve compliance testing</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">GB/T 5330</td>
              <td class="p-3">Chinese industrial wire mesh</td>
              <td class="p-3">China</td>
              <td class="p-3">Equivalent to ISO 9044</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">What to Request from Suppliers</h3>
        <ul class="text-theme-secondary space-y-2">
          <li>✓ Certificate of conformance to ASTM E11 or ISO 9044</li>
          <li>✓ Actual measured mesh count and wire diameter (not nominal)</li>
          <li>✓ Tensile strength test report</li>
          <li>✓ Material composition certificate (mill cert for stainless steel)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 5: Applications -->
    <section id="applications" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">5. Application-Specific Recommendations</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Water Treatment & Filtration</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-2">
            <strong>Recommended:</strong> 304 stainless steel, 40–100 mesh, plain weave
          </p>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            Municipal and industrial water systems need corrosion resistance without chloride exposure. 304 SS is cost-effective. For intake screens in coastal areas, upgrade to 316.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Oil & Gas</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-2">
            <strong>Recommended:</strong> 316 stainless steel, dutch weave, 10–50 μm
          </p>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            High pressure, H2S exposure, and saline produced water demand 316 or duplex stainless. Dutch weave provides absolute filtration for critical applications.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Pharmaceutical & Food</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-2">
            <strong>Recommended:</strong> 316L stainless steel, 100–400 mesh, plain or twill weave
          </p>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            FDA and EU 10/2011 compliance required. 316L's low carbon prevents carbide precipitation during welding. Electropolished mesh reduces particle adhesion and bacterial growth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Chemical Processing</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-2">
            <strong>Recommended:</strong> 316 SS or alloy-specific (Monel, Inconel, Hastelloy)
          </p>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            Match alloy to chemical compatibility charts. For sulfuric acid, use 316 or Hastelloy C-276. For hydrofluoric acid, Monel 400 is required.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Mining & Aggregates</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-2">
            <strong>Recommended:</strong> High-carbon steel or 304 SS, 10–30 mesh, heavy wire
          </p>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            Abrasion resistance matters more than corrosion. Thicker wire (0.5–1.0mm) extends screen life. Consider polyurethane-coated wire for extreme wear.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 6: Sourcing -->
    <section id="sourcing" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">6. Sourcing from Factories: What to Specify</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        When requesting quotes from wire mesh factories (especially in Anping, China), provide these specifications to receive accurate, comparable quotes:
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Minimum Specification Checklist</h3>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Material grade (e.g., 304, 316, 316L)</li>
            <li>☐ Mesh count or micron rating</li>
            <li>☐ Wire diameter (mm or inch)</li>
            <li>☐ Weave type (plain, twill, dutch)</li>
            <li>☐ Roll width and length</li>
          </ul>
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Edge finish (raw, selvage, trimmed)</li>
            <li>☐ Surface treatment (none, electropolished, passivated)</li>
            <li>☐ Tolerance requirements</li>
            <li>☐ Testing standard (ASTM E11, ISO 9044)</li>
            <li>☐ Quantity and packaging</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Red Flags When Evaluating Suppliers</h3>
        <ul class="text-theme-secondary space-y-2 text-sm">
          <li>🚩 Cannot provide material mill certificates</li>
          <li>🚩 Quotes significantly below market rate (may indicate 201 SS substituted for 304)</li>
          <li>🚩 No mention of wire diameter tolerance (±0.01mm is standard; ±0.005mm is precision)</li>
          <li>🚩 Refuses third-party inspection (SGS, Bureau Veritas)</li>
          <li>🚩 No export experience or references</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-6">7. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What is the difference between mesh count and micron rating?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Mesh count = number of wires per linear inch. Micron rating = particle size the mesh can capture. A 100-mesh screen typically filters 150-micron particles, but actual rating depends on wire diameter. Always specify both or use our <a href="/charts/mesh-size-chart/" class="text-theme-primary underline">conversion chart</a>.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Can I use 304 stainless steel for seawater filtration?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            No. 304 will pit and corrode in chloride environments. Use 316 minimum, or better yet, super duplex stainless (2205) or Monel for long-term seawater exposure.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What is open area percentage and why does it matter?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Open area = percentage of screen surface that is open space (not wire). Higher open area = higher flow rate, lower pressure drop. For viscous fluids, specify higher open area (thinner wire or lower mesh count).
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How do I verify a Chinese factory actually uses 316 stainless steel?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Request a mill certificate from the raw material supplier. For critical orders, hire SGS or Bureau Veritas to do PMI (Positive Material Identification) testing with XRF analyzer. This costs $200–500 but prevents $10,000+ mistakes.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What is the typical MOQ for custom filtration mesh?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Standard mesh: 1–5 rolls (30–100m each). Custom weave or special alloy: 100–500m. Dutch weave: often 50–100m minimum. Anping factories are flexible—negotiate based on total order value.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CTA -->
    <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-8 text-center">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Ready to Source Filtration Wire Mesh?</h2>
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-6 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        Get quotes from verified Anping factories with the exact specifications you need. Free inquiry—responses within 24 hours.
